A||N||6||Seanh||Heads||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-30-2001||08:05 PM||||I was wonderin...it has come to my understanding that there are 2 types of 60* V6 heads, large valved and small valved. Large valved ones on the 3.4 and Fiero 2.8...Small valved ones on the other 2.8's and 3.1's. Is that right? If so, are they all universal? If so, does anyone have some of the larger valved Fiero or 3.4L heads laying around they'd like to sell me? I need some...<P>Also, what are the specs on the valves in the 2 diff. heads? <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Primered 86GT w/ 3.4L HO T-6 turbocharged engine :)||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-01-2001||06:36 PM||||Seanh, the "big valved" Fiero heads are as about as common as white bread.  They used them on the 2.8's in mid-80's Camaros, for example.  If I think of it tonight, I'll measure the diameters of the valves and report back to you.<P>If I <B>don't</B> think of it, please harass,  just don't call me a forgetful old man  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>"I came up with a cure for Alzheimer's the other day.  Now, where did i put it? Hmmmm...."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">     ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave Gunsul||11-02-2001||12:50 AM||||The Fiero valve sizes are as follows:<BR>1.72 intake<BR>1.42 exhaust<BR>These heads are not only used on Fieros but all fuel injected 60 degree V6's.<P>The smaller valved heads are found on carbed<BR>or throttle bodied cars and trucks manly from<BR>the early 80's and are as follows:<BR>1.60 intake<BR>1.30 exhaust.<BR>Note: the only carbed engines that the bigged valve heads were used on were the X-11<BR>Citation.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||11-02-2001||01:23 AM||||Same heads were used on the 81-84 carb'd Z engines from the X-body and A body cars. These were the HO V6 engines.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-02-2001||08:07 AM||||Last night I looked in the garage, where I rest my weary heads.  No, I don't sleep out there... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyway, it seems to me the easiest way to tell the "big valved" head by eyeball, is to  notice the gap between the 2 valves, in the middle of the combustion chamber .  On the "big valved" head, the gap between the valves is only about 1/16".  In other words, they're about as big as can be crammed into that combustion chamber.<P>On the "small valved" head, there's about 1/4" gap between the valves.<P>BTW, there are raised characters cast into the head (in the area under the valve cover).       On the "small valved" head, it says, "GM," and under that it <B>looks</B> like it says, "1766505."  The "big valved" head has much smaller characters under the "GM," that <B>look</B> like they say "14054884."<P>The raised numbers were pretty fuzzy, so I probably misread some of the digits.  But with all these clues, you should be able to tell the difference between the 2 heads without any trouble.  At least I hope so!  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Zoom88||11-02-2001||08:51 AM|||| If I am understanding this correctly, are we saying that all year V6 Fieros have the bigger heads (valves) ?||||reg||10
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||11-02-2001||11:08 AM||||Yes all Fiero V6's have the big valves, as does all the other fuel injected 60 degree<BR>V6's.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
